WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.540
In this video, you create an overlay virtual machine network and a Kube-OVN subnet for it. Overlay traffic is tunneled between hosts, so the switches need no VLANs.

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:15.160
Under Networks, Virtual Machine Networks, select Create.

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:18.150
Choose the namespace,

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:20.590
and give the network a name.

00:00:20.590 --> 00:00:28.880
Set the type to OverlayNetwork. An overlay network needs no VLAN ID and no cluster network.

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:31.350
Select Create.

00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:37.950
The network exists, but it has no IP range yet. A Kube-OVN subnet provides one.

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:40.970
Open Overlay Networks,

00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:48.720
and select Virtual Private Cloud. The default VPC, ovn-cluster, holds the built-in subnets.

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:53.310
In the ovn-cluster VPC, select Create Subnet.

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:55.290
Name the subnet.

00:00:55.290 --> 00:01:00.280
Enter a CIDR block that does not overlap with other networks.

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:07.550
As the provider, select the overlay network you just created. This binds the subnet to it.

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:13.500
Leave the gateway IP empty. Kube-OVN uses the first address of the block.

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:20.850
Enable DHCP, so virtual machines on this network receive their addresses automatically.

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:23.300
Select Create.

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:31.130
The subnet is active, with its CIDR block and the overlay network as its provider.

00:01:31.130 --> 00:01:42.071
Virtual machines can now attach to the demo-overlay network and get addresses from its subnet. Next, you hot-plug a network interface.
